  "programming": {
    "id": "fibonacci-clock",
    "definition": {
      "title": "Fibonacci Clock",
      "statement": "## Standard input and output\n\nEach run handles **one** case. Read from standard input and print the result to standard output. Do not read a case count or print case numbers, prompts, or labels. Use the line structure shown in the test-case input below.\n\n### Problem\n\nACSL’s version of Philippe Chretien’s “Fibonacci Clock” displays time by changing the colors displayed in 5 squares, whose side lengths correspond to the first 5 Fibonacci numbers (1, 1, 2, 3, and 5). Given the colors of the squares on the clock face, you must output the time that is represented in hh:mm:ss format. The colors will be given to you as a single string of 5 uppercase characters representing the lower 1x1 square, the upper 1×1 square, the 2×2 square, the 3×3 square, and the 5×5 square in that order. (See https://basbrun.com/2015/05/04/fibonacci-clock/.)\n\nEach square has a color: red, green, blue, cyan, magenta, yellow, or white. All red squares represent only the number of hours; green squares represent only minutes; and blue squares represent only seconds. The intersecting regions in the Venn diagram represent two different values: yellow represents both hours and minutes; magenta represents both hours and seconds; and cyan represents both minutes and seconds. White squares are ignored.\n\n{{diagram:p-34ea6b9a329d1c61}}\n\nFor both minutes and seconds, the sum of the values of the lengths is multiplied by 5 so that the number of minutes and seconds are in intervals of 5 between 0 and 60 inclusive.\n\nHowever, if the number of minutes or seconds is 60 or more and/or if the number of hours is 12 or more, change it to a valid time from 00:00:00 to 11:56:55. For example, 09:60:00 would become 10:00:00 and 12:00:60 would be 00:01:00.\n\nFor example, a clock with a red lower 1×1 square, a white upper 1×1 square, a blue 2×2 square, a green 3×3 square, and a blue 5×5 square displays 01:15:35. The hours are represented by the red 1x1 square. The minutes are represented by the green 3×3 square (3*5=15), and the seconds are represented by the blue 2×2 and the blue 5×5 squares (2+5=7 and 7*5=35).\n\n### Examples\n\n{{diagram:fibonacci-clock-two-examples}}\n\nFor `RWGBG`, the hour is 1, the minutes are $(2+5)\\times5=35$, and the seconds are $3\\times5=15$. The result is `01:35:15`.\n\nFor `RCMGB`, the hours are $1+2=3$, the minutes are $(1+3)\\times5=20$, and the seconds are $(5+1+2)\\times5=40$. The result is `03:20:40`.\n\n### Input\n\nA single five-character uppercase string. Each character is one of `R G B C M Y W`. In order, the characters describe the lower 1×1 square, upper 1×1 square, 2×2 square, 3×3 square, and 5×5 square. The input represents a valid normalized time from `00:00:00` to `11:56:55`.\n\n### Output\n\nPrint the hours, minutes, and seconds in `hh:mm:ss` format. Each component must have two digits.\n",
      "mode": "stdio",
      "comparator": "tokens",
      "timeLimitMs": 2000,
      "memoryLimitMb": 256,
      "starterCode": "# Read one case from standard input.\n# Print the result to standard output.\n",
